1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,924
Two videos ago a release script failed
because it read .git/refs/heads/main and the

2
00:00:09,964 --> 00:00:14,795
ref was packed. A ref is a name, such
as a branch, that holds a commit ID.

3
00:00:14,835 --> 00:00:20,127
Suppose the team patched it: the script
now reads the file if it exists and greps

4
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:25,460
packed-refs otherwise, and it checks that
the result is forty hexadecimal digits.

5
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:31,047
It will fail again, in two new ways. In
one repository, .git/refs/heads isn't a

6
00:00:31,087 --> 00:00:36,603
directory at all. It's a file that says
"this repository uses the reftable format".

7
00:00:36,643 --> 00:00:40,820
In another, the commit ID is
correct and has sixty-four digits.

8
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:46,764
Neither repository is damaged. Both use
storage formats that Git supports today and

9
00:00:46,804 --> 00:00:52,477
plans as defaults for new repositories in
Git 3.0. This video shows both formats and

10
00:00:52,517 --> 00:00:56,897
what stays the same across them.
Keep those two failures in mind.

11
00:00:56,937 --> 00:00:57,917
Both come back.

12
00:00:59,757 --> 00:01:04,028
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:01:04,068 --> 00:01:08,410
Two independent choices are made
when a repository is created.

14
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:14,205
The ref format: how names are stored. The
object format: which hash function names

15
00:01:14,245 --> 00:01:19,011
the objects. A hash function computes
an object's ID from its bytes.

16
00:01:19,051 --> 00:01:24,665
Git 2.55 defaults to files and SHA-1. The
alternatives are reftable and SHA-256.

17
00:01:25,005 --> 00:01:34,120
Three replays from labs/ch03: reftable.sh,
sha256.sh, and the replay of Lab 17.2 up

18
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:36,106
to its checkpoint.

19
00:01:36,446 --> 00:01:41,884
One labelled command is discussed and not
run in the two demos: git refs migrate

20
00:01:41,924 --> 00:01:47,431
--ref-format=reftable, 🟡 CAUTION. It changes
the ref storage format and keeps the

21
00:01:47,471 --> 00:01:52,978
values. Preview it with --dry-run. Recover
by migrating back, or by restoring the

22
00:01:53,018 --> 00:01:54,896
copy of .git you made first.

23
00:01:56,736 --> 00:01:58,148
After this video you can:

24
00:01:58,488 --> 00:02:02,945
Create a repository with the reftable backend
and show what replaces the ref files.

25
00:02:03,285 --> 00:02:06,766
Show that ref plumbing gives the
same answers in both backends.

26
00:02:07,106 --> 00:02:10,884
Say what breaks when tools
read .git/refs directly.

27
00:02:11,224 --> 00:02:17,838
Create a SHA-256 repository and state why
it cannot exchange objects with a SHA-1

28
00:02:17,878 --> 00:02:18,741
repository.

29
00:02:19,081 --> 00:02:24,310
State what Git 3.0 plans for new
repositories, as the section gives it.

30
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:31,815
Reftable first. In one sentence: reftable
stores refs and reflogs in binary,

31
00:02:31,855 --> 00:02:38,046
block-structured tables under .git/reftable/
instead of in loose files, packed-refs

32
00:02:38,086 --> 00:02:44,127
and logs/. It has been available since Git
2.45 and is planned as the default for

33
00:02:44,167 --> 00:02:50,208
new repositories in Git 3.0. A reflog is
the journal of the values a ref has had.

34
00:02:50,548 --> 00:02:53,747
Why does it exist? The
project gives three reasons.

35
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:58,574
The files format can't store two refs
whose names differ only in case on the

36
00:02:58,614 --> 00:03:03,718
case-insensitive filesystems of macOS and
Windows. Deleting a packed ref rewrites

37
00:03:03,758 --> 00:03:07,783
the whole packed-refs file. And
multi-ref updates aren't atomic.

38
00:03:07,823 --> 00:03:12,737
Reftable compacts geometrically after
every write and uses prefix compression.

39
00:03:13,077 --> 00:03:19,182
Now precisely. The tables live in
$GIT_DIR/reftable/, and the file tables.list names

40
00:03:19,222 --> 00:03:25,254
the ones in use, oldest first. A table name
is its first and last update number and

41
00:03:25,294 --> 00:03:31,180
a random suffix. Each table starts with a
24-byte header: REFT, a version byte, a

42
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:35,130
block size, and the range of
update numbers it covers.

43
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:40,827
Two stubs stay for tools that look
for them: .git/HEAD containing ref:

44
00:03:40,867 --> 00:03:45,375
refs/heads/.invalid, and
.git/refs/heads as a regular file.

45
00:03:45,415 --> 00:03:50,231
The format is declared by
extensions.refstorage = reftable with

46
00:03:50,271 --> 00:03:56,398
core.repositoryformatversion = 1, and a
Git that doesn't know the extension must

47
00:03:56,438 --> 00:03:58,094
refuse the repository.

48
00:03:58,434 --> 00:04:04,014
There are three ways in. git init
--ref-format=reftable, or git clone

49
00:04:04,054 --> 00:04:09,960
--ref-format=reftable. The setting
init.defaultRefFormat, since Git 2.47.

50
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,557
Or, for an existing repository, git refs
migrate --ref-format=reftable, since Git

51
00:04:16,597 --> 00:04:23,317
2.46, which can't migrate a repository with
linked worktrees and must not run while

52
00:04:23,357 --> 00:04:25,028
anything else writes.

53
00:04:25,368 --> 00:04:30,232
The ref format is local to each repository.
A files clone fetches from a reftable

54
00:04:30,272 --> 00:04:32,169
server, and the other way round.

55
00:04:32,509 --> 00:04:37,901
Now the object format. In one sentence:
Git names objects with SHA-1 by default,

56
00:04:37,941 --> 00:04:43,264
computed by an implementation that detects
attempted collision attacks, and can

57
00:04:43,304 --> 00:04:48,899
create repositories that use SHA-256 instead.
The two kinds can't exchange objects,

58
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:52,565
and a repository that must
live on GitHub stays SHA-1.

59
00:04:52,605 --> 00:04:56,435
A collision is two different
contents with the same hash.

60
00:04:56,775 --> 00:05:03,441
Since Git 2.13 the default SHA-1 code is
the collision-detecting variant, "SHA1_DC",

61
00:05:03,481 --> 00:05:08,231
which recognises the patterns
of the known attacks on SHA-1.

62
00:05:08,271 --> 00:05:14,537
SHA-256 repositories were experimental
in 2.29 and are a supported format since

63
00:05:14,577 --> 00:05:19,167
2.42. You create one with git
init --object-format=sha256.

64
00:05:19,207 --> 00:05:25,633
It's declared by extensions.objectformat
= sha256, and it's fixed for the life of

65
00:05:25,673 --> 00:05:32,179
the repository: changing the setting
afterwards, the documentation says, "will not

66
00:05:32,219 --> 00:05:35,931
work and will produce
hard-to-diagnose issues".

67
00:05:36,271 --> 00:05:41,799
The format applies to object IDs, the index
checksum and the pack checksums alike.

68
00:05:41,839 --> 00:05:47,367
And the documentation states "no interoperability
between SHA-256 repositories and

69
00:05:47,407 --> 00:05:51,781
SHA-1 repositories". A clone adopts
the format of what it clones.

70
00:05:52,121 --> 00:05:58,636
The Git 3.0 plans, exactly as the chapter
gives them: for new repositories, SHA-256

71
00:05:58,676 --> 00:06:03,138
and reftable as defaults, and
main as the initial branch.

72
00:06:03,178 --> 00:06:09,377
The plan for SHA-256 is conditional on
libraries, applications and forges being

73
00:06:09,417 --> 00:06:15,617
ready, with no plan to deprecate SHA-1.
Git 3.0 is unreleased, and the official

74
00:06:15,657 --> 00:06:17,828
documentation gives no date.

75
00:06:18,168 --> 00:06:24,545
GitHub had no publicly available support
for SHA-256 repositories on 1 October 2026.

76
00:06:24,585 --> 00:06:30,579
A private preview is reported by GitLab's
blog, by a community comment and by a

77
00:06:30,619 --> 00:06:36,843
conference speaker, but no GitHub blog
post, changelog entry or documentation page

78
00:06:36,883 --> 00:06:42,419
announces it, so the course marks it
unverified. Forgejo supports SHA-256

79
00:06:42,459 --> 00:06:48,377
repositories since v7.0; GitLab announced
experimental support in August 2024.

80
00:06:48,417 --> 00:06:54,029
Every repository in this course that
touches GitHub therefore stays SHA-1.

81
00:06:54,369 --> 00:06:58,003
When to adopt reftable: where
it solves a problem you have.

82
00:06:58,043 --> 00:07:03,170
Branch names that collide on a case-insensitive
disk, tens of thousands of refs, or

83
00:07:03,210 --> 00:07:08,151
automation that updates many refs at once.
When not: before you've checked every

84
00:07:08,191 --> 00:07:13,319
tool that opens the repository. A tool built
on an older Git library refuses such a

85
00:07:13,359 --> 00:07:16,743
repository. The repository
is fine. The tool is behind.

86
00:07:18,583 --> 00:07:23,704
The textbook's analogy for reftable: a stack
of ledgers instead of a drawer of index

87
00:07:23,744 --> 00:07:26,838
cards. Every transaction
adds a thin ledger on top.

88
00:07:26,878 --> 00:07:31,876
From time to time thin ledgers are merged
into a thick one, and a lookup reads the

89
00:07:31,916 --> 00:07:33,534
stack from the newest down.

90
00:07:33,874 --> 00:07:38,655
The analogy breaks at correction: a ledger
page could be amended, a table never is.

91
00:07:38,995 --> 00:07:44,024
For both formats, hold one model: the format
is a property of the repository, not of

92
00:07:44,064 --> 00:07:48,067
Git. The names and the objects are
the same things in every format.

93
00:07:48,107 --> 00:07:51,627
What differs is where the bytes
are, and how long an ID is.

94
00:07:51,667 --> 00:07:55,428
Plumbing, Git's low-level commands,
hides the first difference.

95
00:07:55,468 --> 00:07:58,808
git rev-parse --show-object-format
tells you the second.

96
00:08:00,648 --> 00:08:05,678
On the left, what you know: HEAD as a
text file naming a branch, a directory of

97
00:08:05,718 --> 00:08:07,924
loose refs, packed-refs, and logs/.

98
00:08:08,264 --> 00:08:11,594
On the right: HEAD is still
a file, and it's a stub.

99
00:08:11,634 --> 00:08:16,713
refs/heads is a regular file, also a stub.
There's no packed-refs and no logs/.

100
00:08:16,753 --> 00:08:20,536
The refs and the reflogs are
in the tables under reftable/.

101
00:08:20,876 --> 00:08:25,567
The bottom line is the same on both sides:
MERGE_HEAD and FETCH_HEAD are files in

102
00:08:25,607 --> 00:08:28,371
every format. That's what
makes them pseudorefs.

103
00:08:32,711 --> 00:08:38,395
git init is 🟢. Look at the two lines of
the configuration: refstorage = reftable

104
00:08:38,435 --> 00:08:41,972
under extensions, and
repositoryformatversion = 1.

105
00:08:42,312 --> 00:08:47,075
The repository is on main. What
does the file .git/HEAD contain?

106
00:08:47,115 --> 00:08:49,102
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

107
00:08:53,942 --> 00:08:57,527
ref: refs/heads/.invalid.
Neither stub holds a ref.

108
00:08:57,567 --> 00:09:03,000
The real answer comes from Git: git
symbolic-ref HEAD prints refs/heads/main.

109
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:08,829
This is what breaks a tool that reads
.git/refs directly: it finds a file where it

110
00:09:08,869 --> 00:09:12,454
expects a directory, and a
HEAD that names nothing.

111
00:09:12,494 --> 00:09:15,084
That's the first failure of the hook.

112
00:09:15,424 --> 00:09:20,282
The commit wrote update 2, the
branch update 3, the tag update 4.

113
00:09:20,322 --> 00:09:26,537
Auto-compaction had already merged updates
1 to 3 into one table, and git pack-refs

114
00:09:26,577 --> 00:09:32,490
--all merged all four. The header bytes
read REFT, version 1, the block size of

115
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:35,580
4,096 bytes, then update numbers 1 and 4.

116
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:40,667
No logs/, no packed-refs, and every
plumbing command answers as before.

117
00:09:40,707 --> 00:09:44,779
The reflog of the branch is in
the same tables as the branch.

118
00:09:45,119 --> 00:09:48,235
A conflicted merge, to see
where the root refs are kept.

119
00:09:48,575 --> 00:09:54,679
A quick quiz. After the merge stops: which
of MERGE_HEAD, ORIG_HEAD and AUTO_MERGE

120
00:09:54,719 --> 00:09:58,725
will ls -A .git show as files?
All three, two, or one?

121
00:09:58,765 --> 00:09:59,624
Your answer?

122
00:10:07,464 --> 00:10:12,653
One: only MERGE_HEAD. ORIG_HEAD and AUTO_MERGE
are refs and live in the tables, and

123
00:10:12,693 --> 00:10:17,881
git for-each-ref --include-root-refs lists
them. This is the cleanest demonstration

124
00:10:17,921 --> 00:10:20,527
of the root-ref and pseudoref distinction.

125
00:10:20,867 --> 00:10:25,809
Two branches whose names differ only in
case, on a filesystem that ignores case.

126
00:10:25,849 --> 00:10:29,794
In the files repository on the
same disk, the second is refused.

127
00:10:29,834 --> 00:10:34,963
One rule is kept on purpose: feature and
feature/batching can't both exist, so that

128
00:10:35,003 --> 00:10:37,080
repositories stay interchangeable.

129
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:43,751
Both planned formats can be chosen
today, with --object-format=sha256

130
00:10:43,791 --> 00:10:49,106
--ref-format=reftable. Use that
in throwaway repositories.

131
00:10:51,946 --> 00:10:55,836
SHA-1: SHA1_DC: the
collision-detecting implementation.

132
00:10:55,876 --> 00:10:59,481
And the two defaults of
this build: files and sha1.

133
00:10:59,821 --> 00:11:05,553
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
in the lab shell: run git rev-parse

134
00:11:05,593 --> 00:11:09,865
--show-object-format, then git
rev-parse --show-ref-format.

135
00:11:09,905 --> 00:11:10,595
I'll wait.

136
00:11:15,435 --> 00:11:19,335
With the defaults of Git 2.55
you read sha1 and files.

137
00:11:19,375 --> 00:11:22,034
Two words, and you know both formats.

138
00:11:22,374 --> 00:11:26,331
A quick quiz. The same sixteen
bytes in both repositories.

139
00:11:26,371 --> 00:11:30,467
Is the object stored differently,
or only named differently?

140
00:11:30,507 --> 00:11:31,294
Your answer?

141
00:11:36,134 --> 00:11:42,635
Only named differently. The same blob 16,
the same NUL, the same sixteen bytes, and

142
00:11:42,675 --> 00:11:49,018
shasum -a 256 agrees with Git. Everything
in that repository now carries 64-digit

143
00:11:49,058 --> 00:11:52,801
IDs: the tree header of
the commit, and the ref.

144
00:11:53,141 --> 00:11:57,698
The same bytes through the same function,
inside Git or outside it, give the same

145
00:11:57,738 --> 00:11:57,869
ID.

146
00:11:58,209 --> 00:12:03,564
"Mismatched algorithms: client sha1; server
sha256", and in both push directions

147
00:12:03,604 --> 00:12:08,285
"the receiving end does not support
this repository's hash algorithm".

148
00:12:08,325 --> 00:12:13,344
There's no conversion. The fix is to
recreate one side in the other format.

149
00:12:13,684 --> 00:12:18,315
And the second failure of the hook: a check
that assumes forty hexadecimal digits

150
00:12:18,355 --> 00:12:20,909
accepts one repository
and rejects the other.

151
00:12:32,749 --> 00:12:37,562
The lab builds the same history in both
backends. On disk they differ, as in the

152
00:12:37,602 --> 00:12:41,565
diagram. Asked through Git, they
give the same answers, ID for ID.

153
00:12:41,605 --> 00:12:45,993
The lab's failure scenario is a fetch
that collides on a case-insensitive

154
00:12:46,033 --> 00:12:49,936
filesystem, and its recovery uses
the migration. Those are yours.

155
00:12:51,776 --> 00:12:53,424
Five mistakes to watch for.

156
00:12:53,764 --> 00:12:59,836
Reading .git/HEAD or .git/refs in a tool.
Root cause: in a reftable repository both

157
00:12:59,876 --> 00:13:03,076
are stubs and the refs
are in binary tables.

158
00:13:03,416 --> 00:13:06,585
Validating a commit ID as
forty hexadecimal digits.

159
00:13:06,625 --> 00:13:11,807
Root cause: the length is a property of the
repository's object format, not of Git.

160
00:13:12,147 --> 00:13:17,313
Editing extensions.objectFormat or
extensions.refStorage in .git/config.

161
00:13:17,353 --> 00:13:23,386
Root cause: they are set by git init and
git refs migrate; editing them corrupts the

162
00:13:23,426 --> 00:13:24,181
repository.

163
00:13:24,521 --> 00:13:28,269
Trying to push a SHA-256
repository to a SHA-1 remote.

164
00:13:28,309 --> 00:13:33,810
Root cause: there is no interoperability
between the two object formats, and no

165
00:13:33,850 --> 00:13:34,582
conversion.

166
00:13:34,922 --> 00:13:40,112
Migrating a shared repository while it is
in use. Root cause: git refs migrate must

167
00:13:40,152 --> 00:13:44,272
not run while anything else writes,
and linked worktrees block it.

168
00:13:46,112 --> 00:13:50,906
Now, out of the lab. An ML team has
contributors on Linux and on macOS.

169
00:13:50,946 --> 00:13:55,945
Someone on Linux pushes a branch
Fix/tokenizer while fix/tokenizer exists.

170
00:13:55,985 --> 00:14:00,916
On the Macs, git fetch now complains
about a case-insensitive filesystem.

171
00:14:00,956 --> 00:14:06,635
The diagnosis: git ls-remote shows names
that differ only in case, and git rev-parse

172
00:14:06,675 --> 00:14:08,678
and git for-each-ref disagree.

173
00:14:09,018 --> 00:14:14,289
Two fixes are available. Rename a branch
on the server, and add a branch naming

174
00:14:14,329 --> 00:14:19,734
rule. Or migrate the affected clones with
git refs migrate --ref-format=reftable.

175
00:14:19,774 --> 00:14:23,700
The server stays as it is,
because the ref format is local.

176
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:29,026
Before migrating the shared build repository,
the same team checks every tool that

177
00:14:29,066 --> 00:14:33,562
opens it, stops all writers, and
remembers that linked worktrees block the

178
00:14:33,602 --> 00:14:37,668
migration. Their repositories that
are pushed to GitHub stay SHA-1.

179
00:14:39,508 --> 00:14:43,849
Your turn. Do Lab 17.2, "The files
backend versus reftable", in

180
00:14:43,889 --> 00:14:49,481
lab-manual/m17-index-refs-gitdir.md. Before
you list each .git directory, predict

181
00:14:49,521 --> 00:14:54,905
which entries exist in one and not in
the other. Before each plumbing command,

182
00:14:54,945 --> 00:14:58,799
predict whether the two
repositories answer identically.

183
00:14:59,139 --> 00:15:04,476
The challenge is Exercise 16.8,
"Scripts that read .git by hand", in

184
00:15:04,516 --> 00:15:11,118
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md. With this
video you can now answer every part of it,

185
00:15:11,158 --> 00:15:13,965
and Question 81 from the refs video.

186
00:15:15,805 --> 00:15:18,475
Question 86 of the CTO question bank:

187
00:15:18,815 --> 00:15:23,776
A strong answer starts with the problems
that reftable solves and says which of them

188
00:15:23,816 --> 00:15:28,777
the team has. It lists the preconditions of
the migration command and the tools that

189
00:15:28,817 --> 00:15:33,421
must be checked. For "what stays the
same" it names the layer at which nothing

190
00:15:33,461 --> 00:15:37,767
changes and the commands that prove
it. It keeps the Git 3.0 plan a plan.

191
00:15:39,607 --> 00:15:44,281
Let's land this. The format belongs to
the repository, the names and the objects

192
00:15:44,321 --> 00:15:46,108
stay the same, and you ask Git.

193
00:15:46,448 --> 00:15:48,107
You should now be able to say:

194
00:15:48,447 --> 00:15:55,223
Reftable keeps refs and reflogs in binary
tables; .git/HEAD and .git/refs/heads

195
00:15:55,263 --> 00:15:56,603
remain as stubs.

196
00:15:56,943 --> 00:16:01,723
Ref plumbing gives the same answers in
both backends; only the two pseudorefs are

197
00:16:01,763 --> 00:16:02,556
files in both.

198
00:16:02,896 --> 00:16:08,986
Git's SHA-1 implementation detects known
collision attacks; SHA-256 is a separate

199
00:16:09,026 --> 00:16:13,224
object format with 64-digit
IDs and no interoperability.

200
00:16:13,564 --> 00:16:18,658
Decide the object format at git init;
keep repositories for GitHub in SHA-1.

201
00:16:18,998 --> 00:16:25,398
Git 3.0 plans SHA-256 and reftable as
defaults for new repositories, conditionally,

202
00:16:25,438 --> 00:16:26,407
with no date.

203
00:16:28,247 --> 00:16:34,925
Read sections 3.13 to 3.17 of Chapter
3 and do the Practice section 3.19.

204
00:16:35,265 --> 00:16:40,089
Today you met both formats that Git 3.0
plans, and you know what stays the same

205
00:16:40,129 --> 00:16:44,276
across them. Do the lab before the
